<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :paint_borders %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:paint_borders) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Borders and dividers') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every resolver takes an optional <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ el }</code>: the id
            or element whose nearest <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.screen</code> ancestor
            supplies the paint. The probe technique is documented on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border(spec, { dir, el })</code>: a border rail as a
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">BorderFill</code>. The
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">spec</code> is a shade step
              from 10 to 75 or the literal
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'black'</code> /
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'white'</code> rails;
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">dir</code> is
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'h'</code> (default) or
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'v'</code>. Framework borders paint as a background
              on a pseudo-element, so the resolved paint is read there.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">divider({ dir, el })</code>: the level-6
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.divider</code> rail as a
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">BorderFill</code>, probed on the element itself.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Dither-mode lines are multi-layer and list-valued, so
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">BorderFill</code> keeps
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">size</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">position</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">repeat</code> as verbatim computed strings. It also
            carries the renderer-ready SVG program declared by the same CSS rule; JavaScript never parses gradients or
            derives their geometry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: 'BorderFill = {
  color:    string | null,  // resolved background-color (solid/black-white modes)
  image:    string | null,  // resolved background-image gradient(s); null when "none"
  url:      string | null,  // first url(...) ink tile (black/white rails); null otherwise
  size:     string | null,  // VERBATIM background-size string (dither lines are list-valued)
  position: string | null,  // VERBATIM background-position string
  repeat:   string | null,  // VERBATIM background-repeat string
  render: {                  // CSS-declared; copied verbatim by renderer adapters
    stroke:  string | null,
    width:   string | null,
    height:  string | null,
    viewBox: string | null,
    path1:   string | null,
    color1:  string | null,
    path2:   string | null,
    color2:  string | null,
  } | null,
  dir:      "h" | "v",      // which pseudo/orientation was probed
}', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 mt-4">
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border()</code> is the guaranteed-hairline path: it
            probes the fill-backed <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.border--*</code> utilities,
            which carry real ink in every mode and pick up theme tinting for free.
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic('border-strong')</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic('border-muted')</code> describe the semantic
            role's token tile, not the directional rail art. Draw rails with
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border()</code>; reach for
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic('border-*')</code> only when you need the
            role as a Fill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '// Framework rail utilities, resolved for the current mode/theme.
var gridRail = TRMNLPaint.border(65, { el: "my-chart" });     // -> BorderFill
var axisRail = TRMNLPaint.border("black", { dir: "v", el: "my-chart" });
var step40 = TRMNLPaint.border(40, { el: "my-chart" });', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Painting rails') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A resolved rail is written back verbatim, so it paints exactly as the CSS
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.border--*</code> utilities do. Wrap paints in
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">watch()</code> so rails re-resolve on mode, dark and
            theme changes; see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">applyBorder(node, borderFill)</code>: writes a
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">BorderFill</code> onto a node as its five background
              longhands, verbatim, so a solid, a flat gradient and a multi-layer dither line all round-trip unchanged.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Highcharts axes') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The axis adapters copy a resolved rail into Highcharts' native options; the fill adapter and the
            adapter ground rules live on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_charts) %>.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">toHighchartsAxis(borderFill)</code>: turns a
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">BorderFill</code> into a
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ gridLineColor, gridLineWidth, gridLineDashStyle, lineColor, tickColor }</code>
              block.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">applyHighchartsAxisPaint()</code>: carries the
              resolved BorderFill into Highcharts' render cycle and copies its CSS-declared SVG program onto the
              rendered grid, axis and tick paths. Flat modes declare a stroke; patterned modes declare their complete
              tile, paths and colors.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '// Rails into Highcharts axis/grid options.
var grid = TRMNLPaint.toHighchartsAxis(
  TRMNLPaint.border(65, { el: "my-chart" }));
// TRMNLCharts.options() applies the BorderFill pattern to rendered SVG paths.', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Live example') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The separator under each schedule row is painted with
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.border()</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.applyBorder()</code>, wrapped in
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.watch()</code>. Each row uses a different
            spec (shade steps 65 and 40, the literal black and white rails), labelled on the right. Change the
            device mode or Style in the picker and the rails re-resolve, picking up dither art and theme tinting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="<%= framework_example_classes %>">
          <div class="<%= framework_example_inner_classes %>">
            <div class="<%= framework_example_wrapper_classes %>">
              <div class="screen">
                <div class="view view--full">
                  <div class="layout layout--col layout--center">
                    <div id="border-strip" class="flex flex--col w--full"></div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="title_bar">
                    <img class="image image--adaptive" src="/images/plugins/trmnl--render.svg" alt="TRMNL Logo">
                    <span class="title">Paint API</span>
                    <span class="instance">TRMNLPaint.applyBorder</span>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <script type="text/javascript">
                  function whenReady(cb) {
                    var tries = 0;
                    (function attempt() {
                      if (window.TRMNLPaint) return cb();
                      if (++tries > 200) return;
                      setTimeout(attempt, 50);
                    })();
                  }

                  whenReady(function () {
                    var el = "border-strip";
                    var ROWS = [
                      { time: "9:00",  what: "Conference room meeting",   spec: 65 },
                      { time: "11:30", what: "Diversity Day training",    spec: 40 },
                      { time: "14:00", what: "Dundies rehearsal",         spec: "black" },
                      { time: "16:00", what: "Party Planning Committee",  spec: "white" }
                    ];
                    // watch() re-resolves each rail whenever the screen device/scale/mode/dark/theme changes.
                    TRMNLPaint.watch(el, function () {
                      var box = document.getElementById(el);
                      if (!box) return;
                      box.innerHTML = "";
                      ROWS.forEach(function (r) {
                        var row = document.createElement("div");
                        row.className = "flex flex--row flex--center-y gap";
                        row.style.cssText = "padding:12px 0;";
                        var time = document.createElement("span");
                        time.className = "label label--small";
                        time.style.cssText = "width:56px;flex:none;";
                        time.textContent = r.time;
                        var what = document.createElement("span");
                        what.className = "title title--small";
                        what.style.cssText = "flex:1;";
                        what.textContent = r.what;
                        var cap = document.createElement("span");
                        cap.className = "label label--small";
                        cap.textContent = "border(" + JSON.stringify(r.spec) + ")";
                        row.appendChild(time);
                        row.appendChild(what);
                        row.appendChild(cap);
                        var rail = document.createElement("div");
                        rail.style.cssText = "height:2px;width:100%;";
                        // applyBorder writes the five background longhands verbatim, so the
                        // rail paints exactly as the CSS .border--* utilities do.
                        TRMNLPaint.applyBorder(rail, TRMNLPaint.border(r.spec, { el: el }));
                        box.appendChild(row);
                        box.appendChild(rail);
                      });
                    });
                  });
                </script>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '<div id="border-strip" class="flex flex--col w--full"></div>

<script type="text/javascript">
  // plugins.js bundles TRMNLPaint; wait for it before painting.
  function whenReady(cb) {
    var tries = 0;
    (function attempt() {
      if (window.TRMNLPaint) return cb();
      if (++tries > 200) return;
      setTimeout(attempt, 50);
    })();
  }

  whenReady(function () {
    var el = "border-strip";
    var SPECS = [65, 40, "black", "white"];
    // watch() re-resolves each rail whenever the screen device/scale/mode/dark/theme changes.
    TRMNLPaint.watch(el, function () {
      var box = document.getElementById(el);
      if (!box) return;
      box.innerHTML = "";
      SPECS.forEach(function (spec) {
        var row = document.createElement("div");
        row.style.cssText = "padding:12px 0;";
        row.textContent = "border(" + JSON.stringify(spec) + ")";
        var rail = document.createElement("div");
        rail.style.cssText = "height:2px;width:100%;";
        // applyBorder writes the five background longhands verbatim, so the
        // rail paints exactly as the CSS .border--* utilities do.
        TRMNLPaint.applyBorder(rail, TRMNLPaint.border(spec, { el: el }));
        box.appendChild(row);
        box.appendChild(rail);
      });
    });
  });
</script>', language: 'html') %>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
  </div>
</div>
